/frame_2753.jpg", "frames/frame_2754.jpg", "frames/frame_2755.jpg", \n        "frames/frame_2795.jpg", "frames/frame_2796.jpg", "frames/frame_2797.jpg", "frames/frame_2798.jpg", "frames/frame_2799.jpg", "frames/frame_2800.jpg", "frames/frame_2801.jpg", "frames/frame_2802.jpg", "frames/frame_2803.jpg", "frames/frame_2804.jpg", "frames/frame_2805.jpg", \n        "frames/frame_2845.jpg", "frames/frame_2846.jpg", "frames/frame_2847.jpg", "frames/frame_2848.jpg", "frames/frame_2849.jpg", "frames/frame_2850.jpg", "frames/frame_2851.jpg", "frames/frame_2852.jpg", "frames/frame_2853.jpg", "frames/frame_2854.jpg", "frames/frame_2855.jpg", \n        "frames/frame_2895.jpg", "frames/frame_2896.jpg", "frames/frame_2897.jpg", "frames/frame_2898.jpg", "frames/frame_2899.jpg", "frames/frame_2900.jpg", "frames/frame_2901.jpg", "frames/frame_2902.jpg", "frames/frame_2903.jpg", "frames/frame_2904.jpg", "frames/frame_2905.jpg", \n        "frames/frame_2945.jpg", "frames/frame_2946.jpg", "frames/frame_2947.jpg", "frames/frame_2948.jpg", "frames/frame_2949.jpg", "frames/frame_2950.jpg", "frames/frame_2951.jpg", "frames/frame_2952.jpg", "frames/frame_2953.jpg", "frames/frame_2954.jpg", "frames/frame_2955.jpg", \n        "frames/frame_2995.jpg", "frames/frame_2996.jpg", "frames/frame_2997.jpg", "frames/frame_2998.jpg", "frames/frame_2999.jpg", "frames/frame_3000.jpg", "frames/frame_3001.jpg", "frames/frame_3002.jpg", "frames/frame_3003.jpg", "frames/frame_3004.jpg", "frames/frame_3005.jpg"\n    ],\n    "prompt": "Analyze these frames from 45:14-50:00 of a USA vs Australia Olympic basketball game. Verify if answer \'1\' (Because they are genuinely happy) is correct by checking: 1) Are fans showing excited expressions (smiling, cheering, waving)? 2) Are fans holding USA/Australia flags or merchandise? 3) Is basketball game action occurring? 4) Does the scoreboard show game score/time? 5) Are players making impressive plays? 6) Do crowd reactions indicate excitement about specific game events? Determine if fans are excited due to genuine happiness (answer 1) or because of team performances (answers B/C/D). Note the temporal sequence and which team\'s actions trigger crowd reactions."\n}'}, {'role': 'vlm response', 'content': {'batched_responses': [{'batch_start': 0, 'batch_end': 30, 'image_paths': ['/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2709.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2710.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2711.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2712.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2713.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2714.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2715.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2716.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2717.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2718.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2719.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2745.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2746.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2747.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2748.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2749.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2750.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2751.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2752.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2753.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2754.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2755.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2795.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2796.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2797.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2798.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2799.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2800.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2801.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2802.jpg'], 'response': "Based on the analysis of the 30 frames from 45:14–50:00 of the USA vs. Australia Olympic basketball game, **answer '1' (“Because they are genuinely happy”) is NOT correct**. The fans’ excitement is **directly triggered by team performance and game events**, not by a general or abstract sense of “genuine happiness.”\n\nHere’s the verification against each criterion:\n\n---\n\n**1) Are fans showing excited expressions (smiling, cheering, waving)?**  \n✅ Yes — In multiple frames (e.g., Frame 1, 12, 23), spectators are visibly animated — standing, cheering, and waving. Their expressions are reactive and contextual, not passive or ambient.\n\n**2) Are fans holding USA/Australia flags or merchandise?**  \n✅ Yes — In Frame 1, 12, and 23, fans are seen holding flags (including USA flags) and wearing team colors. This indicates partisan support tied to team identity.\n\n**3) Is basketball game action occurring?**  \n✅ Yes — All frames show live gameplay: dribbling, passing, shooting, and defensive plays. The game is in progress during the 2nd quarter (as shown on the scoreboard).\n\n**4) Does the scoreboard show game score/time?**  \n✅ Yes — The scoreboard is visible in nearly every frame (e.g., Frame 1: USA 39, AUS 27, 9:44 2ND). The score updates over time (e.g., Frame 23: USA 39, AUS 31; Frame 30: USA 42, AUS 31), showing progression and competitive tension.\n\n**5) Are players making impressive plays?**  \n✅ Yes — Multiple frames show dynamic actions:  \n- Frame 3: A USA player drives to the basket.  \n- Frame 6–7: USA player #10 (Kevin Durant) takes a jump shot.  \n- Frame 11: USA player #10 celebrates after a successful play.  \n- Frame 20–22: USA player #6 (LeBron James) drives and scores.  \n- Frame 25–27: USA player #13 (Chris Paul) makes a layup.  \nThese are high-impact, crowd-pleasing moments.\n\n**6) Do crowd reactions indicate excitement about specific game events?**  \n✅ Yes — The crowd’s reactions are temporally synchronized with key plays:  \n- After USA scores (e.g., Frame 22, 27), fans are visibly cheering.  \n- In Frame 29, USA bench players (Rudy Gay and David Lee) are clapping and reacting to on-court action.  \n- In Frame 30, LeBron James is shown walking off after scoring, with the scoreboard reflecting a USA lead — this follows a sequence of successful plays that energized the crowd.\n\n---\n\n**Temporal Sequence & Team Triggers:**\n\n- The USA team is consistently scoring and executing high-skill plays (Durant, James, Paul).  \n- Crowd reactions (cheering, standing, waving flags) spike immediately after USA scores or makes a strong defensive play.  \n- For example, between Frame 22 and 27, the USA scores multiple times, and the crowd’s energy visibly increases.  \n- The Australian team’s actions (e.g., Frame 17–18, Australia in possession) do not trigger the same level of crowd reaction — consistent with the crowd being predominantly pro-USA.\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nThe fans’ excitement is **not** due to “genuine happiness” as a general emotional state (answer 1). Instead, it is **directly caused by the performance of the USA team** — their scoring, athleticism, and momentum. The crowd’s reactions are **contextual, reactive, and tied to specific in-game events**.\n\n✅ Therefore, the correct answer is **B or C or D** — depending on the exact wording of those options — but **not 1**.\n\nIf forced to choose among the given options, **answer 1 is incorrect**. The excitement is performance-driven, not abstractly emotional."}, {'batch_start': 30, 'batch_end': 60, 'image_paths': ['/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2803.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2804.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2805.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2845.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2846.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2847.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2848.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2849.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2850.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2851.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2852.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2853.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2854.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2855.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2895.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2896.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2897.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2898.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2899.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2900.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2901.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2902.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2903.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2904.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2905.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2945.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2946.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2947.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2948.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aiem1w_TvaA/frames/frame_2949.jpg'], 'response': 'Based on the analysis of the 30 frames from 45:14–50:00 of the USA vs. Australia Olympic basketball game, the answer **‘1’ (Because they are genuinely happy)** is **not correct**.\n\nHere’s the verification based on the six criteria:\n\n---\n\n**1) Are fans showing excited expressions (smiling, cheering, waving)?**  \n→ **No.** The frames do not show close-ups or clear shots of fans exhibiting excited expressions. The crowd is visible in wide shots (e.g., Frames 2, 8, 16), but their individual expressions are not discernible. There is no visible cheering or waving captured in these specific frames.\n\n**2) Are fans holding USA/Australia flags or merchandise?**  \n→ **No.** While the arena branding and team jerseys are visible, there is no clear evidence in these frames of fans holding flags or wearing team-specific merchandise. The focus is on the court, players, and coaches.\n\n**3) Is basketball game action occurring?**  \n→ **Yes.** Game action is clearly occurring — players are dribbling, passing, defending, and shooting (e.g., Frames 8–14, 16–22). The game is in the 2nd quarter with active play.\n\n**4) Does the scoreboard show game score/time?**  \n→ **Yes.** The scoreboard is visible in multiple frames (e.g., 1, 4, 8, 15, 16, 23, 26), showing USA 42–31 (later 44–36) and time remaining around 5:13 to 4:31 in the 2nd quarter. This confirms the game is live and progressing.\n\n**5) Are players making impressive plays?**  \n→ **Partially.** While there are active plays (e.g., drives to the basket, defensive rotations), no explicitly “impressive” or highlight-reel plays (like dunks, blocks, or long-range threes) are captured in these frames. The play is competitive but routine.\n\n**6) Do crowd reactions indicate excitement about specific game events?**  \n→ **No.** The crowd is present and appears engaged, but no specific reactions (e.g., sudden cheers, standing ovations, or emotional reactions) are captured in these frames. The focus is on the players and coaching huddles, not crowd reactions.\n\n---\n\n**Temporal Sequence & Team Triggers:**\n\n- The frames begin with LeBron James (USA) walking on court (Frame 1), followed by wide shots of gameplay (Frames 2–3, 8–14).\n- The scoreboard updates show USA increasing their lead (from 42–31 to 44–36).\n- Australian players (e.g., #8, #7) are shown reacting — looking tired, frustrated, or focused (Frames 4–7, 23–25).\n- An Australian coach is shown giving instructions during a timeout (Frames 26–30), indicating a strategic moment rather than a crowd-pleasing event.\n\n→ **No crowd reaction is shown to be triggered by a specific play or team action.** The frames are more focused on the game’s flow and coaching strategy than fan excitement.\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nThe fans’ excitement (if any) is not captured in these frames. The visible energy comes from the **players’ competitive actions** and **coaching intensity**, not from fans expressing “genuine happiness.” The context suggests that any excitement would be tied to **team performance** (USA extending lead, Australia fighting back), not abstract happiness.\n\n✅ Therefore, **answer ‘1’ is incorrect**. The correct interpretation would be that any fan excitement (not shown here) would stem from **team performances** — aligning with answers **B/C/D** (depending on the exact options, which are not fully provided, but clearly not ‘1’).\n\n**Final Answer: ‘1’ is not correct.**'}, {'batch_start': 60, 'batch_end': 77, 'image_paths': ['/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/Aie